{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a38abacd9040dafa", "type": "tool_result", "content": "299\t\n300\tSec. 3.3 shows that models with comparable Accuracy can still differ substantially across MMAC dimensions. Gemini 2.5 Pro performs better on implication and dynamic, indicating stronger performance in inferring implicit meanings and describing temporal changes. In contrast, Qwen3-Omni-Captioner provides broader Coverage on descriptive dimensions such as content and paralinguistic information. This suggests that a single aggregate score cannot fully capture how models differ across dimensions.\n301\t\n302\tFig. 5: Effect of caption length on MMAC evaluation.\n303\t\n304\tThe gap between coverage and reliability is also reflected in model training and output length. Comparing the two Qwen3-Omni models, supervised fine-tuning for captioning substantially improves Coverage, but does not lead to consistent Precision gains. Implication is the only dimension where both Coverage and Accuracy decrease, suggesting that a stronger tendency to describe more information does not necessarily improve implicit meaning inference. We further control the output length of Gemini 2.5 Pro by adding target word-count constraints to the original prompt. As shown in Fig. 5, longer captions cover more target information, but Precision decreases as length increases. These results show that detailed audio captioning should be evaluated in terms of both information coverage and description reliability.\n305\t\n306\t4 Conclusion and future work\n307\t\n308\tIn this paper, we presented MMAC, a multi-dimensional benchmark for audio captioning. MMAC comprises 6 capability categories and 15 fine-grained dimensions and evaluates the coverage and correctness of target information in free-form captions. Results reveal clear differences among AudioLLMs in information coverage and description reliability. Analyses of captioning-oriented supervised fine-tuning and output length further show that broader coverage does not necessarily lead to more reliable descriptions. MMAC provides a fine-grained basis for model comparison, error analysis, and future captioning model development. One limitation is that our human evaluation used pre-annotations generated by Qwen3.6-27B, which may have introduced anchoring bias. Therefore, the close agreement between the scores produced by Qwen3.6-27B and the human ratings should not be interpreted as evidence that this judge is superior to the alternatives. Future work will adopt independent annotations, extend MMAC’s coverage of languages, scenarios, and dimensions, and introduce timestamp-based evaluation for more precise temporal localization.\n309\t\n310\tReferences\n311\t\n312\t[1]\n313\tY. Bai, X. Du, Y. Liang, et al. (2025)\n314\t\n315\tCoig-cqia: quality is all you need for chinese instruction fine-tuning.\n316\t\n317\tIn Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: NAACL 2025,\n318\t\n319\t pp. 8190–8205.\n320\t\n321\tCited by: §2.3.\n322\t\n323\t[2]\n324\tG. Chen, S. Chai, and G. o. Wang (2021)\n325\t\n326\tGigaSpeech: an evolving, multi-domain asr corpus with 10,000 hours of transcribed audio.\n327\t\n328\tIn Interspeech,\n329\t\n330\t pp. 3670–3674.\n331\t\n332\tCited by: §2.3.\n333\t\n334\t[3]\n335\tW. Cui, D. Yu, X. Jiao, et al. (2025)\n336\t\n337\tRecent advances in speech language models: a survey.\n338\t\n339\tIn Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers),\n340\t\n341\t pp. 13943–13970.\n342\t\n343\tCited by: §2.3.\n344\t\n345\t[4]\n346\tH. Dinkel, G. Li, J. Liu, et al. (2025)\n347\t\n348\tMidashenglm: efficient audio understanding with general audio captions.\n349\t\n350\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2508.03983.\n351\t\n352\tCited by: §1.\n353\t\n354\t[5]\n355\tK. Drossos, S. Lipping, and T. Virtanen (2020)\n356\t\n357\tClotho: an audio captioning dataset.\n358\t\n359\tIn ICASSP 2020-2020 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP),\n360\t\n361\t pp. 736–740.\n362\t\n363\tCited by: §1.\n364\t\n365\t[6]\n366\tE. Fonseca, X. Favory, J. Pons, et al. (2021)\n367\t\n368\tFsd50k: an open dataset of human-labeled sound events.\n369\t\n370\tIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing 30, pp. 829–852.\n371\t\n372\tCited by: §2.3.\n373\t\n374\t[7]\n375\tJ. F. Gemmeke, D. P. Ellis, et al. (2017)\n376\t\n377\tAudio set: an ontology and human-labeled dataset for audio events.\n378\t\n379\tIn 2017 IEEE international conference on acoustics, speech and signal processing (ICASSP),\n380\t\n381\t pp. 776–780.\n382\t\n383\tCited by: §2.3.\n384\t\n385\t[8]\n386\tS. Ghosh, A. Goel, K. Jayakumar, L. Koroshinadze, N. Anand, Z. Kong, S. Gururani, S. Lee, J. Kim, A. Aljafari, et al. (2026)\n387\t\n388\tAudio flamingo next: next-generation open audio-language models for speech, sound, and music.\n389\t\n390\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2604.10905.\n391\t\n392\tCited by: §2.2.\n393\t\n394\t[9]\n395\tY. Huang, X. Lin, Z. Liu, et al.\n396\t\n397\tMUSTARD: mastering uniform synthesis of theorem and proof data.\n398\t\n399\tIn The Twelfth International Conference on Learning Representations,\n400\t\n401\tCited by: §2.3.\n402\t\n403\t[10]\n404\tJ. H. Jin Xu et al. (2025)\n405\t\n406\tQwen2.5-omni technical report.\n407\t\n408\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2503.20215.\n409\t\n410\tCited by: §1.\n411\t\n412\t[11]\n413\tM. G. Kendall and B. B. Smith (1939)\n414\t\n415\tThe problem of mm rankings.\n416\t\n417\tAnnals of Mathematical Statistics 10 (3), pp. 275–287.\n418\t\n419\tCited by: §3.3.\n420\t\n421\t[12]\n422\tC. D. Kim, B. Kim, H. Lee, et al. (2019)\n423\t\n424\tAudiocaps: generating captions for audios in the wild.\n425\t\n426\tIn Proceedings of the 2019 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies, Volume 1 (Long and Short Papers),\n427\t\n428\t pp. 119–132.\n429\t\n430\tCited by: §1.\n431\t\n432\t[13]\n433\tZ. Ma, Y. Ma, Y. Zhu, et al. (2026)\n434\t\n435\tMmar: a challenging benchmark for deep reasoning in speech, audio, music, and their mix.\n436\t\n437\tAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems 38.\n438\t\n439\tCited by: §1.\n440\t\n441\t[14]\n442\tZ. Ma, R. Xu, Z. Xing, et al. (2026)\n443\t\n444\tOmni-captioner: data pipeline, models, and benchmark for omni detailed perception.\n445\t\n446\tIn The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations,\n447\t\n448\tCited by: §1.\n449\t\n450\t[15]\n451\tX. Mei, C. Meng, H. Liu, et al. (2024)\n452\t\n453\tWavcaps: a chatgpt-assisted weakly-labelled audio captioning dataset for audio-language multimodal research.\n454\t\n455\tIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing 32, pp. 3339–3354.\n456\t\n457\tCited by: §1.\n458\t\n459\t[16]\n460\tK. J. Piczak (2015)\n461\t\n462\tESC: dataset for environmental sound classification.\n463\t\n464\tIn Proceedings of the 23rd ACM international conference on Multimedia,\n465\t\n466\t pp. 1015–1018.\n467\t\n468\tCited by: §2.3.\n469\t\n470\t[17]\n471\tS. Sakshi, U. Tyagi, S. Kumar, et al. (2025)\n472\t\n473\tMmau: a massive multi-task audio understanding and reasoning benchmark.\n474\t\n475\tIn International Conference on Learning Representations,\n476\t\n477\tVol. 2025, pp. 84929–84964.\n478\t\n479\tCited by: §1.\n480\t\n481\t[18]\n482\tZ. Tang, D. Wang, Y. Xu, et al. (2021)\n483\t\n484\tKespeech: an open source speech dataset of mandarin and its eight subdialects.\n485\t\n486\tIn Thirty-fifth conference on neural information processing systems datasets and benchmarks track,\n487\t\n488\tCited by: §2.3.\n489\t\n490\t[19]\n491\tQ. Team (2026)\n492\t\n493\tQwen3. 5-omni technical report.\n494\t\n495\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2604.15804.\n496\t\n497\tCited by: §1.\n498\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4f8b14b1-7207-437d-b74d-23cdf831f62d", "uuid": "7b432086-cc37-45c4-87ae-7772aee3a15b", "timestamp": "2026-07-30T06:52:06.724Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/mmac_text.txt", "content": "\nSec. 3.3 shows that models with comparable Accuracy can still differ substantially across MMAC dimensions. Gemini 2.5 Pro performs better on implication and dynamic, indicating stronger performance in inferring implicit meanings and describing temporal changes. In contrast, Qwen3-Omni-Captioner provides broader Coverage on descriptive dimensions such as content and paralinguistic information. This suggests that a single aggregate score cannot fully capture how models differ across dimensions.\n\nFig. 5: Effect of caption length on MMAC evaluation.\n\nThe gap between coverage and reliability is also reflected in model training and output length. Comparing the two Qwen3-Omni models, supervised fine-tuning for captioning substantially improves Coverage, but does not lead to consistent Precision gains. Implication is the only dimension where both Coverage and Accuracy decrease, suggesting that a stronger tendency to describe more information does not necessarily improve implicit meaning inference. We further control the output length of Gemini 2.5 Pro by adding target word-count constraints to the original prompt. As shown in Fig. 5, longer captions cover more target information, but Precision decreases as length increases. These results show that detailed audio captioning should be evaluated in terms of both information coverage and description reliability.\n\n4 Conclusion and future work\n\nIn this paper, we presented MMAC, a multi-dimensional benchmark for audio captioning. MMAC comprises 6 capability categories and 15 fine-grained dimensions and evaluates the coverage and correctness of target information in free-form captions. Results reveal clear differences among AudioLLMs in information coverage and description reliability. Analyses of captioning-oriented supervised fine-tuning and output length further show that broader coverage does not necessarily lead to more reliable descriptions. MMAC provides a fine-grained basis for model comparison, error analysis, and future captioning model development. One limitation is that our human evaluation used pre-annotations generated by Qwen3.6-27B, which may have introduced anchoring bias. Therefore, the close agreement between the scores produced by Qwen3.6-27B and the human ratings should not be interpreted as evidence that this judge is superior to the alternatives. Future work will adopt independent annotations, extend MMAC’s coverage of languages, scenarios, and dimensions, and introduce timestamp-based evaluation for more precise temporal localization.\n\nReferences\n\n[1]\nY. Bai, X. Du, Y. Liang, et al. (2025)\n\nCoig-cqia: quality is all you need for chinese instruction fine-tuning.\n\nIn Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: NAACL 2025,\n\n pp. 8190–8205.\n\nCited by: §2.3.\n\n[2]\nG. Chen, S. Chai, and G. o. Wang (2021)\n\nGigaSpeech: an evolving, multi-domain asr corpus with 10,000 hours of transcribed audio.\n\nIn Interspeech,\n\n pp. 3670–3674.\n\nCited by: §2.3.\n\n[3]\nW. Cui, D. Yu, X. Jiao, et al. (2025)\n\nRecent advances in speech language models: a survey.\n\nIn Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers),\n\n pp. 13943–13970.\n\nCited by: §2.3.\n\n[4]\nH. Dinkel, G. Li, J. Liu, et al. (2025)\n\nMidashenglm: efficient audio understanding with general audio captions.\n\narXiv preprint arXiv:2508.03983.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[5]\nK. Drossos, S. Lipping, and T. Virtanen (2020)\n\nClotho: an audio captioning dataset.\n\nIn ICASSP 2020-2020 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP),\n\n pp. 736–740.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[6]\nE. Fonseca, X. Favory, J. Pons, et al. (2021)\n\nFsd50k: an open dataset of human-labeled sound events.\n\nIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing 30, pp. 829–852.\n\nCited by: §2.3.\n\n[7]\nJ. F. Gemmeke, D. P. Ellis, et al. (2017)\n\nAudio set: an ontology and human-labeled dataset for audio events.\n\nIn 2017 IEEE international conference on acoustics, speech and signal processing (ICASSP),\n\n pp. 776–780.\n\nCited by: §2.3.\n\n[8]\nS. Ghosh, A. Goel, K. Jayakumar, L. Koroshinadze, N. Anand, Z. Kong, S. Gururani, S. Lee, J. Kim, A. Aljafari, et al. (2026)\n\nAudio flamingo next: next-generation open audio-language models for speech, sound, and music.\n\narXiv preprint arXiv:2604.10905.\n\nCited by: §2.2.\n\n[9]\nY. Huang, X. Lin, Z. Liu, et al.\n\nMUSTARD: mastering uniform synthesis of theorem and proof data.\n\nIn The Twelfth International Conference on Learning Representations,\n\nCited by: §2.3.\n\n[10]\nJ. H. Jin Xu et al. (2025)\n\nQwen2.5-omni technical report.\n\narXiv preprint arXiv:2503.20215.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[11]\nM. G. Kendall and B. B. Smith (1939)\n\nThe problem of mm rankings.\n\nAnnals of Mathematical Statistics 10 (3), pp. 275–287.\n\nCited by: §3.3.\n\n[12]\nC. D. Kim, B. Kim, H. Lee, et al. (2019)\n\nAudiocaps: generating captions for audios in the wild.\n\nIn Proceedings of the 2019 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies, Volume 1 (Long and Short Papers),\n\n pp. 119–132.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[13]\nZ. Ma, Y. Ma, Y. Zhu, et al. (2026)\n\nMmar: a challenging benchmark for deep reasoning in speech, audio, music, and their mix.\n\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems 38.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[14]\nZ. Ma, R. Xu, Z. Xing, et al. (2026)\n\nOmni-captioner: data pipeline, models, and benchmark for omni detailed perception.\n\nIn The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations,\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[15]\nX. Mei, C. Meng, H. Liu, et al. (2024)\n\nWavcaps: a chatgpt-assisted weakly-labelled audio captioning dataset for audio-language multimodal research.\n\nIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing 32, pp. 3339–3354.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[16]\nK. J. Piczak (2015)\n\nESC: dataset for environmental sound classification.\n\nIn Proceedings of the 23rd ACM international conference on Multimedia,\n\n pp. 1015–1018.\n\nCited by: §2.3.\n\n[17]\nS. Sakshi, U. Tyagi, S. Kumar, et al. (2025)\n\nMmau: a massive multi-task audio understanding and reasoning benchmark.\n\nIn International Conference on Learning Representations,\n\nVol. 2025, pp. 84929–84964.\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n[18]\nZ. Tang, D. Wang, Y. Xu, et al. (2021)\n\nKespeech: an open source speech dataset of mandarin and its eight subdialects.\n\nIn Thirty-fifth conference on neural information processing systems datasets and benchmarks track,\n\nCited by: §2.3.\n\n[19]\nQ. Team (2026)\n\nQwen3. 5-omni technical report.\n\narXiv preprint arXiv:2604.15804.\n\nCited by: §1.\n", "numLines": 200, "startLine": 299, "totalLines": 682}}}
